Task 1: Import an OVA File into OpenShift Virtualization
Subtask 1.1: Prepare the Import Environment
First, let's verify that OpenShift Virtualization is properly installed and ready for VM imports.

Access the OpenShift Web Console

Open your web browser and navigate to the OpenShift console
Log in with your provided credentials
Verify Virtualization Operator Status

oc get csv -n openshift-cnv
Check Available Storage Classes

oc get storageclass
Create a Dedicated Namespace for VM Imports

oc new-project vm-import-lab
Subtask 1.2: Download and Prepare Sample OVA File
For this lab, we'll use a sample Ubuntu OVA file that's commonly available.

Navigate to the VM Import Directory

cd /home/lab-user/vm-imports
Verify OVA File Availability

ls -la *.ova
Extract OVA Contents for Inspection

tar -tf ubuntu-sample.ova
Subtask 1.3: Import OVA Using the Web Console
Navigate to Virtualization Section

In the OpenShift web console, click on Virtualization in the left navigation
Select Virtual Machines
Start the Import Process

Click Create Virtual Machine
Select Import via URL or Upload
Configure Import Settings

Name: imported-ubuntu-vm
Description: Ubuntu VM imported from OVA format
Operating System: Ubuntu 20.04 LTS
Flavor: Small (1 CPU, 2 GiB Memory)
Subtask 1.4: Import OVA Using CLI Method
Alternatively, you can import using the command line interface:

Create VM Import Configuration

cat << EOF > vm-import-config.yaml
apiVersion: v2v.kubevirt.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualMachineImport
metadata:
  name: ubuntu-import
  namespace: vm-import-lab
spec:
  providerCredentialsSecret:
    name: ova-credentials
  source:
    ova:
      url: "file:///home/lab-user/vm-imports/ubuntu-sample.ova"
  targetVmName: imported-ubuntu-vm
  startVm: false
EOF
Apply the Import Configuration

oc apply -f vm-import-config.yaml
Monitor Import Progress

oc get virtualmachineimport ubuntu-import -w
Task 2: Configure Network and Storage Options for the Imported VM
Subtask 2.1: Configure Network Settings
Review Current Network Configuration

oc get vm imported-ubuntu-vm -o yaml | grep -A 10 networks
Create Custom Network Attachment Definition

cat << EOF > custom-network.yaml
apiVersion: k8s.cni.cncf.io/v1
kind: NetworkAttachmentDefinition
metadata:
  name: vm-network
  namespace: vm-import-lab
spec:
  config: |
    {
      "cniVersion": "0.3.1",
      "type": "bridge",
      "bridge": "br1",
      "ipam": {
        "type": "static",
        "addresses": [
          {
            "address": "192.168.100.10/24"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
EOF
Apply Network Configuration

oc apply -f custom-network.yaml
Update VM Network Configuration

oc patch vm imported-ubuntu-vm --type='merge' -p='{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"networks":[{"name":"default","pod":{}},{"name":"vm-network","multus":{"networkName":"vm-network"}}]}}}}'
Subtask 2.2: Configure Storage Options
Check Current Storage Configuration

oc get vm imported-ubuntu-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.volumes}' | jq
Create Additional Storage Volume

cat << EOF > additional-storage.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolumeClaim
metadata:
  name: vm-data-disk
  namespace: vm-import-lab
spec:
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  resources:
    requests:
      storage: 10Gi
  storageClassName: standard
EOF
Apply Storage Configuration

oc apply -f additional-storage.yaml
Attach Additional Storage to VM

oc patch vm imported-ubuntu-vm --type='json' -p='[{"op": "add", "path": "/spec/template/spec/volumes/-", "value": {"name": "data-disk", "persistentVolumeClaim": {"claimName": "vm-data-disk"}}}, {"op": "add", "path": "/spec/template/spec/domain/devices/disks/-", "value": {"name": "data-disk", "disk": {"bus": "virtio"}}}]'
Subtask 2.3: Configure VM Resources
Update CPU and Memory Allocation

oc patch vm imported-ubuntu-vm --type='merge' -p='{"spec":{"template":{"spec":{"domain":{"cpu":{"cores":2},"memory":{"guest":"4Gi"}}}}}}'
Verify Resource Configuration

oc get vm imported-ubuntu-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.domain.cpu.cores}'
oc get vm imported-ubuntu-vm -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.domain.memory.guest}'
Task 3: Verify VM Functionality Post-Import
Subtask 3.1: Start and Monitor the Imported VM
Start the Virtual Machine

oc patch vm imported-ubuntu-vm --type='merge' -p='{"spec":{"running":true}}'
Monitor VM Startup Process

oc get vmi imported-ubuntu-vm -w
Check VM Status

oc get vm imported-ubuntu-vm
oc get vmi imported-ubuntu-vm
Subtask 3.2: Access and Test VM Functionality
Get VM Console Access

virtctl console imported-ubuntu-vm
Alternative: Use VNC Console

virtctl vnc imported-ubuntu-vm
Test Network Connectivity from VM

Log into the VM console
Run network tests:
# Inside the VM
ip addr show
ping -c 4 8.8.8.8
curl -I http://www.google.com
Subtask 3.3: Validate Storage Configuration
Check Disk Configuration Inside VM

# Inside the VM console
lsblk
df -h
Test Additional Storage Disk

# Inside the VM
sudo fdisk -l
sudo mkfs.ext4 /dev/vdb
sudo mkdir /mnt/data
sudo mount /dev/vdb /mnt/data
echo "Test file" | sudo tee /mnt/data/test.txt
cat /mnt/data/test.txt
Subtask 3.4: Performance and Health Verification
Check VM Resource Usage

oc top vmi imported-ubuntu-vm
Verify VM Events

oc get events --field-selector involvedObject.name=imported-ubuntu-vm
Test VM Migration Capability

oc patch vmi imported-ubuntu-vm --type='merge' -p='{"spec":{"evictionStrategy":"LiveMigrate"}}'
Monitor VM Health Status

oc get vmi imported-ubuntu-vm -o jsonpath='{.status.conditions}' | jq
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Import Failures
Issue: OVA import fails with storage errors Solution:

# Check storage class availability
oc get storageclass
# Verify PVC creation
oc get pvc -n vm-import-lab
# Check storage provisioner logs
oc logs -n kube-system -l app=storage-provisioner
Issue: Network configuration problems Solution:

# Verify network attachment definitions
oc get network-attachment-definitions -n vm-import-lab
# Check CNI plugin status
oc get pods -n openshift-multus
VM Startup Issues
Issue: VM fails to start after import Solution:

# Check VM events
oc describe vm imported-ubuntu-vm
# Verify virtualization components
oc get pods -n openshift-cnv
# Check node resources
oc describe nodes
